# 1
out.append(Paragraph('Exit Liquidity Lab',styles['Title']));para('Proportional withdrawals when a vault is short on cash<br/>Maxine Koh Xin Yi',True)
h('Introduction')
para('Exit Liquidity Lab asks how a vault should split the cash it actually has when several people want to withdraw at once. The project implements proportional funding in Solidity and compares it with queue-based policies. A local test token and a fixture with controlled return times make it possible to repeat the same shortage.')
para('In the synthetic comparison, proportional settlement puts some cash in reach of every requester at the first checkpoint. FIFO completes the first withdrawal sooner. In either case, the same amount of liquidity returns.')
h('Problem Statement')
para('Lending and yield vault depositors can own claims on assets that are not immediately available. Borrowers may be using the assets, or strategies may require time and operational actions to unwind. Aave documents that withdrawal access depends on unborrowed liquidity; at full utilization, suppliers wait for repayments or additional supply [1]. A balance therefore expresses an entitlement, not an unconditional promise of immediate cash.')
para('The engineering problem is how to record competing exit requests, allocate recovered funds under a published policy and distinguish pending entitlements from funded claims. Ordinary sequential withdrawals can make outcomes sensitive to transaction order. A proportional policy reduces that sensitivity within one cohort, at the cost of batching delay and partial rather than complete payments.')
para('The target users are depositors and vault operators evaluating constrained exits. The contribution is an implemented allocation experiment and explicit accounting, not a novel lending protocol or a claim that established protocols lack withdrawal mechanisms. A conventional database could record requests, but enforcing pooled-token transfers on-chain allows participants to verify permissions and allocation rules independently of the backend.')
#2
page();h('Evidence and Objectives')
sub('Protocol evidence and historical cases')
para('Heimbach, Schertenleib and Wattenhofer study Aave and Compound around the September 2022 Ethereum Merge. They document Aave ETH utilization reaching 100% and lenders losing withdrawal access as available liquidity was exhausted, despite intervention restricting borrowing. The study reports no accompanying spike in mass liquidations or irretrievable loans [2]. This supports separating liquidity constraints from insolvency; it does not establish that this prototype would have prevented the event.')
para('A second governance record identifies SNX utilization reaching 100% in Aave V2 during November 2022 [3]. That retrospective establishes another instance of exhausted unborrowed liquidity, but does not establish individual waiting times or failed transaction counts. Neither case is replayed by the experiment: the scenarios below are synthetic.')
para('Sun, Stasinakis and Sermpinis examine available liquidity and market concentration in Aave [4]. Their academic preprint supports examining concentrated request sizes rather than relying only on aggregate deposits. The IMF also identifies utilization spikes and concentrated liquidity provision in its historical sample [5]. These sources motivate the stress conditions, rather than prove that one allocation policy is universally superior.')
sub('Existing mechanisms and scope')
para('Morpho already documents liquidity constraints and configuration-dependent exit mechanisms [6]. Yearn uses a withdrawal queue to order strategy liquidation; this differs from a queue of depositor requests [7]. ERC-7540 specifies asynchronous request and claim flows [8]. The prototype borrows its lifecycle vocabulary but is not ERC-4626 or ERC-7540 compliant.')
table([['Objective','Observable acceptance evidence'],['Financial correctness','Allocations never exceed funded tokens; claims cannot repeat'],['Complete DApp','Deposit, request, close, settle and claim through the web interface'],['Transparent state','Separate unrequested principal, pending demand and claimable funds'],['Evaluation','Identical cash-return path for sequential, FIFO and proportional models'],['Reproducibility','Pinned dependencies, scripts, JSON results and genuine local screenshots']],[110,385])
para('Scope is one six-decimal test token, one vault, one fixture and one on-chain allocation policy. There is no leverage, oracle, swap, performance fee, strategy loss or interest accrual. The restricted scope isolates allocation correctness while preserving a complete financial transaction lifecycle.')
#3
page();h('System Architecture')
table([['Layer','Responsibility and trust boundary'],['Browser and MetaMask','Displays balances and asks the wallet to sign. Wallet controls user keys.'],['Node.js API','Reads configuration, contract state and confirmed events. Holds no signing keys.'],['ExitVault','Enforces requests, timing, proportional entitlement, reservations and claims.'],['DemoToken','Six-decimal faucet asset with no monetary value.'],['LiquidityFixture','Holds deployed tokens; manager controls return timing, destination fixed to vault.']],[115,380])
para('Transaction path: browser action → MetaMask signature → Ethereum JSON-RPC → contract execution → receipt → refreshed UI. Read path: browser → Node API → RPC reads or event scan → JSON. Contract state is authoritative. The backend cannot approve withdrawals or change allocations; its failure affects visibility, not recorded ownership.')
para('The manager can deploy free funds only before any current-cohort request exists. Anyone can close an expired round or settle a closed round. Settlement reserves available tokens for existing requests. Each depositor claims to the address that created the request. The fixture manager can withhold returns indefinitely, making its liquidity assumption explicit rather than suggesting autonomous investment performance.')
sub('Technologies Used')
para('Solidity 0.8.38 compiles for the Shanghai EVM with optimizer enabled for 200 runs. Ethers 6.17.0 constructs calls and reads receipts. Ganache 7.9.2 supplies a deterministic local Ethereum environment. Node.js provides a minimal HTTP backend; the frontend uses HTML, CSS and JavaScript modules. A package lock pins dependency resolution. No database is required because confirmed contract events reconstruct activity.')
para('Verification used local chain ID 1337. The browser test exercised the wallet interface through an injected provider. Testing with the MetaMask extension and deployment on Sepolia are still open.')
para('The API exposes /api/config, /api/state and /api/history. History scans at most 5,000 recent blocks and declares truncation. Re-reading events after refresh avoids presenting transient browser memory as permanent transaction history. A production indexer would need pagination, persistence and chain-reorganization handling.')
#4
page();h('Smart Contract Design')
para('A round accepts requests until its immutable close timestamp. Requesting transfers an amount from a user\'s unrequested principal balance into the round ledger; it does not transfer tokens out. After closure, the total request denominator is fixed. Settlement may occur repeatedly as funds return. Full funding opens the next round even if earlier claimants have not collected their reserved tokens.')
para('For request q, closed-round total Q, cumulative funding F and cumulative amount already paid P, the next claim is floor(q × F / Q) - P. All quantities are token base units. F never exceeds Q. Cumulative calculation avoids losing fractional entitlement after repeated partial settlements: when F = Q, the original q is exactly payable.')
table([['Function','Principal rule'],['deposit(a)','Positive amount; exact token balance increase; credit principal'],['request(a)','Open window and sufficient unrequested entitlement'],['close()','Timestamp reached; freeze cohort; advance if empty'],['settle()','Closed cohort; reserve min(free liquidity, unmet demand)'],['claim(round)','Only caller entitlement; update accounting before transfer'],['deploy(a)','Manager only; no current requests; never use reservations']],[105,390])
para('Free liquidity equals the vault token balance minus global reservations. Reserved tokens are excluded from future settlement and deployment. Principal decreases only when a claim transfers tokens out. With no direct donations, losses or unsupported token behavior, outstanding principal equals tokens held by the vault plus fixture. Donations create excess assets rather than new user principal.')
para('Security invariants are: reserved ≤ vault balance; funded ≤ requested; paid(user) ≤ cumulative entitlement(user); and a user cannot request against the same principal twice. State-changing functions use a reentrancy guard, and checked arithmetic rejects overflow. Each settle and claim uses a fixed number of storage operations; no participant enumeration is required. This is a structural complexity observation, not a measured large-scale throughput result.')
para('There are deliberate restrictions. Only one cohort is active; later users cannot request until it is fully funded. Pending requests cannot be cancelled. Settlement is permissionless but still needs a transaction. A total principal cap of 10^18 base units bounds q and F, so their product is at most 10^36, below uint256 capacity. General token compatibility and production solvency are not claimed.')
#5
page();h('Implementation')
para('The frontend connects a wallet, checks its network, and reloads on account or chain changes. Deposits require an exact-amount token approval followed by a separate deposit signature. A submitted hash is shown as pending; success is displayed only after a receipt with successful status. Rejections and execution errors produce an action-not-completed message. Round controls remain contract-authorized even when visible to all users.')
