sub('Security privacy and ethics')
para('Authorization rests on msg.sender, not a backend session. Public reads require no login; there are no protected backend mutation endpoints. The backend stores no wallet keys. Public addresses and transactions are observable and pseudonymous, not private. No names, financial documents or personal identifiers are placed on-chain. The interface labels the faucet asset and absence of actual yield. Testing is not an audit, and no real-money suitability is asserted.',True)
#6
page();h('Testing and Results')
para(f'The regression runner passed {tests["passed"]} named assertions. An independent state-machine oracle checked {stress["steps"]} actions across six seeds, including {stress["reverts"]} mined reverts and {stress["assertions"]:,} assertions. A separate 32-depositor test used 17 funding installments and recovered every base unit after full funding. These bounded randomized and scenario tests are not exhaustive fuzzing or a formal proof.')
para('A separate headless Chrome run exercised connection, faucet mint, approval, deposit, fixture deployment, withdrawal request, closure, settlement and claim against the local HTTP backend and chain. A 100-token deposit with 60 deployed produced a 40-token partial claim and 60 outstanding principal. The history API returned confirmed events; the browser recorded no page errors. Responsive desktop and mobile captures are included. Six HTTP cases and four simulated wallet-error cases also passed. Actual extension testing and human usability remain unverified.')
table([['Operation','Last observed gas'],['Deposit',gas['deposit']],['Settle',gas['settle']],['Claim',gas['claim']]],[250,245])
para('Gas values are last-observed successful local transactions from the test runner, not averages. Storage state differs between operations; there is no production fee or throughput inference. Source: evidence/tests.json. No statistically meaningful confirmation-latency study was conducted.',True)
sub('Synthetic policy comparison')
para('Three users request 100, 150 and 250 tokens. Returned liquidity is 200, then 100, then 200 at three abstract checkpoints. FIFO serves arrival order; sequential service uses reverse order. Both allow partial withdrawals. The integer cumulative model assumes prompt claims and checks 901 scenario/permutation pairs, including no return, excess return and zero demand. It does not model strategic arrivals or cross-cohort welfare.')
table([['Policy','Checkpoint 1 fulfilled percent','Full completion checkpoints'],['Sequential reverse order','0 / 0 / 80','3 / 3 / 2'],['FIFO arrival order','100 / 66.67 / 0','1 / 2 / 3'],['Proportional cohort','40 / 40 / 40','3 / 3 / 3']],[125,185,185])
para('All policies return the same aggregate cash. Proportional service removes within-cohort order dependence in this example and gives everyone an initial allocation, but completes no user early. FIFO benefits earlier users. The experiment supports a distributional trade-off, not universal fairness, better yield or faster liquidity creation. Source: evidence/benchmark.json.')
#7
page();h('Challenges Encountered')
para('The main design challenge was preserving entitlements across repeated partial funding. Per-settlement rounding can obscure residual claims; cumulative funding with per-user paid balances makes remaining entitlement explicit. Global reservation accounting prevents an unclaimed allocation from being reused. Browser approval and deposit are separate transactions, so the interface communicates each stage rather than implying one atomic action.')
para('Local execution also exposed transaction gas-estimation sensitivity when the same function encountered a different storage branch. The test harness uses explicit gas limits for affected branch transitions and still checks successful receipts. This is an implementation lesson; deployed gas use is measured independently of the supplied limit. The local chain used Ganache\'s JavaScript fallback under Node 24, so wall-clock performance is not representative of production.')
h('Limitations')
para('The fixture models temporary illiquidity with eventual recovery assumed in completed scenarios. The manager can delay returns permanently. The mechanism cannot resolve default or distinguish recoverable debt from bad assets. It guarantees only allocation of tokens actually reserved. A new deposit can supply cash for existing exits but creates a corresponding new principal liability; this is liquidity substitution, not a solvency improvement.')
para('A closed cohort blocks later requests until fully funded, introducing cross-cohort priority and possible starvation. All exits are delayed by the request window, even when cash exists. No cancellation path exists. Malicious-token behavior, public-network reorgs, user research and exhaustive adversarial campaigns remain incomplete. Local provider testing does not establish actual MetaMask or Sepolia acceptance. The portfolio adapter and interest/loss accounting have not been implemented. Dependency review retains 33 advisories, including five critical, in the Ganache development subtree; the runtime dependency was upgraded. The local chain is test-only and must not hold real keys.')
h('Future Improvements')
para('First complete actual MetaMask acceptance and Sepolia evidence. Next add a single lending adapter on a pinned fork, then share-based yield accounting with an explicit rule for gains and losses while requests wait. Compare idle-liquidity buffers under stated yield assumptions. Introduce independently reviewed full-precision arithmetic and broader invariant/fuzz tests before increasing economic complexity. A persistent event indexer should reconcile reorgs and paginate history.')
h('Conclusion')
para('Spreading each funding installment across the round gives all requesters earlier access to some of their money in this example. FIFO gets the earliest requester out sooner. The contract makes its choice explicit, tracks funded claims, and leaves the timing of future returns to the fixture. That timing is the main limitation for later rounds.')
